+# The Daily Grind
+
+Requires Node.js 20 or later. `npm start` serves the homepage and newsletter API
+on `PORT` (default `11222`), bound to `HOST` (default `0.0.0.0`). No dependencies
+or build output are needed. `npm run build` checks JavaScript syntax without
+starting the server.
+
+Newsletter signups are accepted by `POST /api/newsletter` as JSON or a regular
+HTML form. Valid email addresses and subscription timestamps are appended to
+`data/newsletter.jsonl`. Set `NEWSLETTER_FILE` to an absolute path on persistent
+private storage in production. This collects subscriptions; it does not send
+newsletter campaigns. The server serves only the homepage, stylesheet, and
+client script, never the signup file.
+
+Keep the enforcing `Connection-Allowlist` and `Content-Security-Policy` response
+headers when deploying behind a proxy, and serve the production site over HTTPS.
+The connection allowlist permits the response origin and exactly
+`https://images.unsplash.com`; redirects and WebRTC retain their default blocks.
+It is sent on every response, so it also covers document and worker requests
+without varying on `Sec-Fetch-Dest`. Workers are additionally disabled by CSP.
+
+CSP restricts fetches and form submissions to this server, allows Unsplash only
+for images, and authorizes the newsletter script with a fresh nonce per response.
+Styles are external so inline styles and handlers need no exceptions.
+Browsers without Connection-Allowlist support still enforce CSP, but CSP cannot
+cover every network channel (including external top-level navigation and WebRTC).
+See the [Connection Allowlists specification](https://wicg.github.io/connection-allowlists/)
+for the network-layer policy and its browser support limitations.
+
+The form shows success only after storage succeeds, preserves input on failure,
+and handles HTTP errors, blocked requests, offline failures, and a ten-second
+timeout. Without JavaScript, the same endpoint returns a readable confirmation
+or error page without using a redirect.
